      "id": "hackernews-2026-05-26-language-models-need-sleep",
      "title": "Language Models Need Sleep",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.26099\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48281226\nPoints: 121\n# Comments: 92",
      "body": "Abstract:Transformer-based large language models are increasingly used for long-horizon tasks; however, their attention mechanism scales poorly with context length. To handle this, we study a sleep-like consolidation mechanism in which a model periodically converts recent context into persistent fast weights before clearing its key-value cache. During sleep, the model performs $N$ offline recurrent passes over the accumulated context and updates the fast weights in its state-space model (SSM) blocks through a learned local rule. During inference, this shifts extra computation to sleep while preserving the latency of wake-time prediction. We test our method on controlled synthetic tasks, including cellular automata and multi-hop graph retrieval, as well as a realistic math reasoning task, on which a regular transformer as well as SSM-attention hybrid models fail. We then show that increasing sleep duration $N$ for our models improves performance, with the largest gains on examples that require deeper reasoning.\nSubmission history From: Sangyun Lee [view email]          [v1]\nMon, 25 May 2026 17:55:39 UTC (319 KB)",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "juxtapose",
      "publishedAt": "2026-05-26T15:36:24.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "/static/browse/0.3.4/images/arxiv-logo-fb.png",
